            "function": {
              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"file_text\": \"# .NET Runtime in .NET 11 Preview 3\\n\\n## Runtime Async Enabled by Default\\n\\n> This is a preview feature for .NET 11.\\n\\nIn Preview 2, runtime async required explicit opt-in through a compiler feature flag and `<EnablePreviewFeatures>`. In Preview 3, runtime async is **enabled by default for `net11.0` targets** ([dotnet/runtime #125406](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125406)). No project file changes are needed.\\n\\nThe runtime manages async suspension and resumption directly, producing cleaner stack traces, better debugger integration, and lower overhead than the compiler-generated state machine approach.\\n\\n```xml\\n<!-- Preview 2 required this; Preview 3 does not -->\\n<PropertyGroup>\\n  <Features>runtime-async=on</Features>\\n  <EnablePreviewFeatures>true</EnablePreviewFeatures>\\n</PropertyGroup>\\n```\\n\\nTo opt out of runtime async for a specific method, apply `[MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.NoInlining)]` or set `<Features>runtime-async=off</Features>` at the project level.\\n\\n### JIT optimizations for runtime async\\n\\nPreview 3 includes two JIT improvements that reduce overhead for runtime-async methods:\\n\\n- **Continuation reuse** — the JIT now reuses continuation objects across await boundaries in common patterns, cutting allocation pressure in tight async loops.\\n- **Unmutated-local skipping** — locals that are never modified after capture no longer need to be stored and restored around await points, reducing stack spill and resume cost.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ML-KEM Stabilized\\n\\nPost-quantum cryptography with ML-KEM (Module-Lattice Key Encapsulation Mechanism) is now a stable API in .NET 11 ([dotnet/runtime #125735](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125735)). The `[Experimental]` attribute has been removed.\\n\\n```csharp\\nusing System.Security.Cryptography;\\n\\n// Key encapsulation\\nusing var kem = MLKem.GenerateKey(MLKemAlgorithm.MLKem768);\\nbyte[] encapsulationKey = kem.ExportEncapsulationKey();\\n\\n// Encapsulate (sender side)\\nbyte[] ciphertext, sharedSecret;\\nMLKem.Encapsulate(encapsulationKey, out ciphertext, out sharedSecret);\\n\\n// Decapsulate (recipient side)\\nbyte[] decapsulatedSecret = kem.Decapsulate(ciphertext);\\n```\\n\\nML-KEM is part of the NIST post-quantum cryptography standards (FIPS 203). It joins the stable API surface alongside existing asymmetric key types like RSA and ECDSA.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## GC Regions on macOS\\n\\nThe GC regions feature, which improves memory reclamation granularity and reduces heap fragmentation, is now enabled on **macOS** ([dotnet/runtime #125416](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125416)). GC regions divide the heap into fixed-size segments that can be individually reclaimed, yielding lower working-set sizes for apps with uneven allocation patterns. This is transparent — no configuration changes required.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## New Process APIs\\n\\n### Process signals and handle-based process management\\n\\nThree new additions improve low-level process management:\\n\\n**`Process.Signal`** ([dotnet/runtime #126313](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/126313)) — sends an arbitrary signal to a process on Unix:\\n\\n```csharp\\nusing System.Diagnostics;\\n\\nvar proc = Process.Start(\\\"my-daemon\\\");\\nproc.Signal(Signum.SIGUSR1); // send SIGUSR1\\n```\\n\\n**`SafeProcessHandle.Start` and `SafeProcessHandle.ProcessId`** ([dotnet/runtime #126192](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/126192)) — launch a process and retrieve its ID through a `SafeHandle`, enabling robust lifetime management without the race condition present in `Process.Id`:\\n\\n```csharp\\nvar startInfo = new ProcessStartInfo(\\\"my-app\\\") { UseShellExecute = false };\\nusing SafeProcessHandle handle = SafeProcessHandle.Start(startInfo);\\nint pid = handle.ProcessId;\\n```\\n\\n**`StandardInputHandle`, `StandardOutputHandle`, `StandardErrorHandle` on `ProcessStartInfo`** ([dotnet/runtime #125848](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125848)) — redirect standard streams to existing handles without pipes:\\n\\n```csharp\\nvar psi = new ProcessStartInfo(\\\"cmd\\\")\\n{\\n    StandardInputHandle = existingInputHandle,\\n    StandardOutputHandle = existingOutputHandle,\\n    StandardErrorHandle = existingErrorHandle\\n};\\nProcess.Start(psi);\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## RegexOptions.AnyNewLine\\n\\nA new `RegexOptions.AnyNewLine` flag makes `^`, `$`, and `.` treat all common newline sequences — `\\\\r`, `\\\\n`, and `\\\\r\\\\n` — as line breaks ([dotnet/runtime #124701](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124701)). Previously, these patterns only recognized `\\\\n` by default.\\n\\n```csharp\\nusing System.Text.RegularExpressions;\\n\\n// Matches lines starting with '#' in files with any line ending\\nvar regex = new Regex(@\\\"^#.+$\\\", RegexOptions.Multiline | RegexOptions.AnyNewLine);\\nvar matches = regex.Matches(content);\\n```\\n\\nThis is particularly useful when processing text from different platforms or network protocols where the line-ending convention is not guaranteed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## JIT Performance\\n\\n### `Span.SequenceEqual` constant folding\\n\\nThe JIT now constant-folds `Span<T>.SequenceEqual` calls when both operands are compile-time constants ([dotnet/runtime #125800](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/issues/125800)). Hot paths that compare known byte sequences — such as HTTP header name matching — can eliminate the comparison entirely.\\n\\n### `Dictionary.Remove` inlining\\n\\n`Dictionary<TKey, TValue>.Remove` is now inlined in more call sites ([dotnet/runtime #126100](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/issues/126100)), reducing per-call overhead in code that removes items frequently.\\n\\n### `HashSet.Contains` bounds check elimination\\n\\nThe JIT eliminates redundant bounds checks in `HashSet<T>.Contains` ([dotnet/runtime #125900](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/issues/125900)), giving a small throughput improvement for lookup-heavy code paths.\\n\", \"path\": \"/home/runner/work/dotnet-core-release-notes/dotnet-core-release-notes/release-notes/11.0/preview/preview3/runtime.md\"}"
