            "type": "function",
            "function": {
              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"file_text\": \"# .NET Libraries in .NET 11 Preview 3\\n\\nHere's a summary of what's new in .NET libraries in this preview release:\\n\\n- [System.Text.Json improvements](#systemtextjson-improvements)\\n- [MemoryCache OpenTelemetry metrics](#memorycache-opentelemetry-metrics)\\n- [Configuration: ChainedConfigurationSource reload](#configuration-chainedconfigurationsource-reload)\\n- [ZIP extraction CRC32 validation](#zip-extraction-crc32-validation)\\n- [SafeFileHandle improvements](#safefilehandle-improvements)\\n- [Floating-point hex formatting](#floating-point-hex-formatting)\\n- [Console FORCE_COLOR support](#console-force_color-support)\\n- [Unsafe Evolution runtime attributes](#unsafe-evolution-runtime-attributes)\\n\\n## System.Text.Json Improvements\\n\\nPreview 3 delivers a significant batch of `System.Text.Json` improvements.\\n\\n### `JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase`\\n\\nA new `JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase` value joins the existing `CamelCase`, `SnakeCaseLower`, `SnakeCaseUpper`, `KebabCaseLower`, and `KebabCaseUpper` policies ([dotnet/runtime #124644](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124644)).\\n\\n```csharp\\nvar options = new JsonSerializerOptions\\n{\\n    PropertyNamingPolicy = JsonNamingPolicy.PascalCase\\n};\\nstring json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new { firstName = \\\"Alice\\\", lastName = \\\"Smith\\\" }, options);\\n// {\\\"FirstName\\\":\\\"Alice\\\",\\\"LastName\\\":\\\"Smith\\\"}\\n```\\n\\n### `[JsonNamingPolicy]` attribute for member-level policies\\n\\nA new `[JsonNamingPolicy]` attribute overrides the naming policy for individual properties, so types that mix conventions don't need a custom converter ([dotnet/runtime #124645](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124645)):\\n\\n```csharp\\npublic class Response\\n{\\n    public string Id { get; set; }\\n\\n    [JsonNamingPolicy(typeof(JsonCamelCaseNamingPolicy))]\\n    public string DisplayName { get; set; }  // serialized as \\\"displayName\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Type-level `[JsonIgnore]` with condition\\n\\n`[JsonIgnore]` can now be placed on a type (class, struct, record) with a `Condition` to apply the ignore behavior to every property automatically ([dotnet/runtime #124646](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124646)):\\n\\n```csharp\\n[JsonIgnore(Condition = JsonIgnoreCondition.WhenWritingNull)]\\npublic class ApiResponse\\n{\\n    public string? ErrorMessage { get; set; }\\n    public string? WarningMessage { get; set; }\\n    public string Data { get; set; } = \\\"\\\";\\n}\\n// Null ErrorMessage and WarningMessage are omitted from the output\\n```\\n\\n### Generic converters on generic types\\n\\n`[JsonConverter]` attributes on generic type definitions now apply correctly when the type is instantiated with concrete type arguments ([dotnet/runtime #123209](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/123209)). Previously, a converter applied to `MyType<T>` had no effect when serializing `MyType<int>`.\\n\\n### Byref constructor parameters\\n\\n`System.Text.Json` can now deserialize into types whose constructors take `ref` or `out` parameters ([dotnet/runtime #122950](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/122950)), enabling deserialization into high-performance value types that use byref initialization.\\n\\n### F# discriminated union serialization\\n\\nF# discriminated unions serialize and deserialize correctly without custom converters ([dotnet/runtime #125610](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125610)):\\n\\n```fsharp\\ntype Shape =\\n    | Circle of radius: float\\n    | Rectangle of width: float * height: float\\n\\nlet circle = Circle 3.0\\nlet json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(circle)\\n// {\\\"Case\\\":\\\"Circle\\\",\\\"Fields\\\":[3.0]}\\n```\\n\\n### `KeyNotFoundException` includes property name\\n\\nWhen a required JSON property is missing during deserialization, the `KeyNotFoundException` message now includes the property name ([dotnet/runtime #124965](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124965)), making debugging deserialization failures faster.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## MemoryCache OpenTelemetry Metrics\\n\\n`IMemoryCache` now emits built-in OpenTelemetry metrics without any additional instrumentation libraries ([dotnet/runtime #126146](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/126146)):\\n\\n| Metric | Description |\\n|--------|-------------|\\n| `dotnet.memory_cache.hits` | Cache hit count |\\n| `dotnet.memory_cache.misses` | Cache miss count |\\n| `dotnet.memory_cache.size` | Current number of entries |\\n| `dotnet.memory_cache.removals` | Entries removed (eviction + expiry) |\\n\\n```csharp\\nbuilder.Services.AddMemoryCache();\\nbuilder.Services.AddOpenTelemetry()\\n    .WithMetrics(metrics => metrics\\n        .AddMeter(\\\"Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Memory\\\")\\n        .AddConsoleExporter());\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Configuration: ChainedConfigurationSource Reload\\n\\n`ChainedConfigurationSource` now supports reload tokens ([dotnet/runtime #125122](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125122)). Previously, wrapping a reloadable configuration source (such as `appsettings.json`) in a `ChainedConfigurationSource` silently dropped reload notifications, breaking `IOptionsMonitor<T>` and `IOptionsSnapshot<T>` in multi-configuration scenarios.\\n\\n```csharp\\n// Reload now propagates through chained sources\\nvar builder = new ConfigurationBuilder()\\n    .AddJsonFile(\\\"appsettings.json\\\", reloadOnChange: true);\\n\\nvar chainedSource = new ChainedConfigurationSource\\n{\\n    Configuration = builder.Build(),\\n    ShouldDisposeConfiguration = true\\n};\\n\\nvar finalConfig = new ConfigurationBuilder()\\n    .Add(chainedSource)\\n    .Build();\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ZIP Extraction CRC32 Validation\\n\\n`ZipFile.ExtractToDirectory` and related APIs now validate the CRC32 checksum of each entry during extraction ([dotnet/runtime #124766](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124766)). Corrupt or tampered archive entries throw `InvalidDataException` before writing partial data to disk, rather than silently producing bad output.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## SafeFileHandle Improvements\\n\\n### Anonymous pipes with async I/O\\n\\n`SafeFileHandle.CreateAnonymousPipe` creates anonymous pipe handles that support overlapped (async) I/O ([dotnet/runtime #125220](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125220)). Previously, creating async-capable pipes required platform-specific workarounds.\\n\\n```csharp\\nvar (readHandle, writeHandle) = SafeFileHandle.CreateAnonymousPipe();\\nusing var reader = new FileStream(readHandle, FileAccess.Read, bufferSize: 4096, isAsync: true);\\nusing var writer = new FileStream(writeHandle, FileAccess.Write, bufferSize: 4096, isAsync: true);\\n```\\n\\n### `FileHandleType` and `SafeFileHandle.Type`\\n\\n`SafeFileHandle.Type` returns a `FileHandleType` enum value describing the underlying kernel object ([dotnet/runtime #124561](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124561)):\\n\\n```csharp\\nusing var handle = File.OpenHandle(\\\"output.log\\\");\\nConsole.WriteLine(handle.Type); // FileHandleType.Disk, Pipe, Character, or Unknown\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Floating-Point Hex Formatting\\n\\n`float` and `double` values can now be formatted in C99 hexadecimal format (`%a`/`%A`) using the `\\\"H\\\"` format specifier ([dotnet/runtime #124139](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124139)). Hex float notation preserves all bits without rounding:\\n\\n```csharp\\ndouble value = Math.PI;\\nConsole.WriteLine(value.ToString(\\\"H\\\")); // 0x1.921fb54442d18p+1\\nConsole.WriteLine(value.ToString(\\\"h\\\")); // 0x1.921fb54442d18p+1 (lowercase)\\n```\\n\\nThis is useful for graphics code, scientific computing, and cross-language interop where exact float representation matters.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Console FORCE_COLOR Support\\n\\n`Console` now respects the `FORCE_COLOR` environment variable ([dotnet/runtime #124492](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124492)). When `FORCE_COLOR=1` (or `2`, `3`), ANSI escape codes are enabled even when stdout is not attached to a terminal — for example, when piped to a file or another process in CI environments.\\n\\n```bash\\nFORCE_COLOR=1 dotnet run | tee output.log\\n```\\n\\nThis follows the [FORCE_COLOR specification](https://force-color.org/) used by Node.js, Deno, and many CLI tools.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Unsafe Evolution Runtime Attributes\\n\\n> This is a preview feature for .NET 11.\\n\\nThe runtime now includes attributes that support the [Unsafe Evolution](./csharp.md#unsafe-evolution) language feature in C# 14 ([dotnet/runtime #125721](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125721)). These attributes allow library authors to annotate APIs with memory-safety requirements that the compiler enforces based on `LangVersion`. Primarily relevant to low-level library authors; most application code is unaffected.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Community Contributors\\n\\nThank you to all community contributors who had PRs merged in .NET 11 Preview 3:\\n\\n<!-- Community contributors will be listed here once the full PR list is available from the release tooling. -->\\n\", \"path\": \"/home/runner/work/dotnet-core-release-notes/dotnet-core-release-notes/release-notes/11.0/preview/preview3/libraries.md\"}"
