              "arguments": "{\"file_text\": \"# ASP.NET Core in .NET 11 Preview 3 - Release Notes\\n\\nHere's a summary of what's new in ASP.NET Core in this preview release:\\n\\n- [Zstandard response compression](#zstandard-response-compression)\\n- [HTTP/3 connection startup latency improvement](#http3-connection-startup-latency-improvement)\\n- [OpenBSD RID support](#openbsd-rid-support)\\n\\nASP.NET Core updates in .NET 11:\\n\\n- [What's new in ASP.NET Core in .NET 11](https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/release-notes/aspnetcore-11) documentation.\\n- [Roadmap](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/issues/64787)\\n\\n## Zstandard Response Compression\\n\\nASP.NET Core now supports **Zstandard (zstd)** as a response compression algorithm ([dotnet/aspnetcore #65479](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/65479)). zstd provides better compression ratios than gzip and Brotli at similar CPU budgets, and is natively supported by all major browsers and HTTP clients.\\n\\nTo enable zstd compression alongside Brotli and gzip:\\n\\n```csharp\\nbuilder.Services.AddResponseCompression(options =>\\n{\\n    options.Providers.Add<ZstandardCompressionProvider>();\\n    options.Providers.Add<BrotliCompressionProvider>();\\n    options.Providers.Add<GzipCompressionProvider>();\\n    options.EnableForHttps = true;\\n});\\n\\napp.UseResponseCompression();\\n```\\n\\nThe `ZstandardCompressionProvider` respects the standard `Accept-Encoding: zstd` request header and negotiates the algorithm automatically. Compression level is configurable:\\n\\n```csharp\\nbuilder.Services.Configure<ZstandardCompressionProviderOptions>(options =>\\n{\\n    options.Level = CompressionLevel.Optimal;\\n});\\n```\\n\\nClients that do not send `Accept-Encoding: zstd` continue to receive Brotli or gzip responses, so adding zstd is backward compatible.\\n\\n## HTTP/3 Connection Startup Latency Improvement\\n\\nKestrel's HTTP/3 implementation now reduces connection startup latency ([dotnet/aspnetcore #65399](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/65399)). The improvement is most visible in services that handle many short-lived HTTP/3 connections — such as microservice-to-microservice calls and API gateways — where connection establishment represents a significant share of total latency.\\n\\nNo configuration changes are needed to benefit from this improvement.\\n\\n## OpenBSD RID Support\\n\\nASP.NET Core packages now include OpenBSD runtime identifiers ([dotnet/aspnetcore #65628](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/65628)), enabling .NET 11 web applications to run on OpenBSD without custom RID configuration.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Community Contributors\\n\\nThank you to all community contributors who had PRs merged in ASP.NET Core in .NET 11 Preview 3:\\n\\n<!-- Community contributors will be listed here once the full PR list is available from the release tooling. -->\\n\", \"path\": \"/home/runner/work/dotnet-core-release-notes/dotnet-core-release-notes/release-notes/11.0/preview/preview3/aspnetcore.md\"}"
            }
          }
        ],
