            "function": {
              "name": "create",
              "arguments": "{\"file_text\": \"# Entity Framework Core in .NET 11 Preview 3\\n\\nHere's a summary of what's new in Entity Framework Core in this preview release:\\n\\n- [Cosmos DB: complex properties support](#cosmos-db-complex-properties-support)\\n- [Partial property loading in relational queries](#partial-property-loading-in-relational-queries)\\n- [ChangeTracker.GetEntriesForState()](#changetrackergetentriesforstate)\\n- [Parameterless AddPooledDbContextFactory](#parameterless-addpooledddbcontextfactory)\\n- [Better SQL for optional one-to-one joins](#better-sql-for-optional-one-to-one-joins)\\n- [EF.Functions.JsonContains for SQL Server 2025](#effunctionsjsoncontains-for-sql-server-2025)\\n- [Exclude foreign keys from migrations](#exclude-foreign-keys-from-migrations)\\n- [RemoveExtension and RemoveDbContext APIs](#removeextension-and-removedbcontext-apis)\\n- [Migration ID recorded in model snapshot](#migration-id-recorded-in-model-snapshot)\\n\\nFor more information on these changes, see the [Entity Framework Core 11.0 what's new](https://learn.microsoft.com/ef/core/what-is-new/ef-core-11.0/whatsnew) documentation.\\n\\n## Cosmos DB: Complex Properties Support\\n\\nEF Core now supports **complex properties** in Azure Cosmos DB — both in model building and in LINQ queries.\\n\\n### Model building\\n\\nComplex types can be configured as embedded properties in a Cosmos document ([dotnet/efcore #37919](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37919)):\\n\\n```csharp\\npublic class Order\\n{\\n    public int Id { get; set; }\\n    public string CustomerName { get; set; } = \\\"\\\";\\n    public Address ShippingAddress { get; set; } = new();\\n}\\n\\npublic class Address\\n{\\n    public string Street { get; set; } = \\\"\\\";\\n    public string City { get; set; } = \\\"\\\";\\n    public string PostalCode { get; set; } = \\\"\\\";\\n}\\n\\nmodelBuilder.Entity<Order>()\\n    .ComplexProperty(o => o.ShippingAddress);\\n```\\n\\n### Query support\\n\\nComplex properties can now be used in LINQ predicates and projections against Cosmos ([dotnet/efcore #37577](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37577)):\\n\\n```csharp\\nvar seattleOrders = await context.Orders\\n    .Where(o => o.ShippingAddress.City == \\\"Seattle\\\")\\n    .ToListAsync();\\n\\nvar addressSummaries = await context.Orders\\n    .Select(o => o.ShippingAddress)\\n    .ToListAsync();\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Partial Property Loading in Relational Queries\\n\\nRelational providers now support loading a subset of scalar properties from a navigation without a full `Include` ([dotnet/efcore #37857](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37857)). This is useful when you need only one or two fields from a related entity and don't want the overhead of loading the full object.\\n\\n```csharp\\n// Load only the customer's email from the related Order\\nvar orderSummaries = await context.Orders\\n    .Select(o => new\\n    {\\n        o.Id,\\n        CustomerEmail = o.Customer.Email  // partial load — no full Customer Include needed\\n    })\\n    .ToListAsync();\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## ChangeTracker.GetEntriesForState()\\n\\n`ChangeTracker.GetEntriesForState(EntityState state)` returns all tracked entries in a given state ([dotnet/efcore #37847](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37847)), replacing the previous pattern of filtering `ChangeTracker.Entries()` manually:\\n\\n```csharp\\n// Before\\nvar modified = context.ChangeTracker.Entries()\\n    .Where(e => e.State == EntityState.Modified);\\n\\n// After\\nvar modified = context.ChangeTracker.GetEntriesForState(EntityState.Modified);\\n```\\n\\nA generic overload lets you filter by entity type at the same time:\\n\\n```csharp\\nvar modifiedOrders = context.ChangeTracker\\n    .GetEntriesForState<Order>(EntityState.Modified);\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Parameterless AddPooledDbContextFactory\\n\\n`AddPooledDbContextFactory<TContext>()` can now be called without an explicit configuration action when the `DbContext` configuration is already registered in the DI container ([dotnet/efcore #37144](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37144)):\\n\\n```csharp\\n// Register the connection string separately\\nbuilder.Services.AddSingleton<IDbContextOptionsConfiguration<AppDbContext>>(\\n    _ => new MyDbContextConfiguration());\\n\\n// No lambda needed — options come from DI\\nbuilder.Services.AddPooledDbContextFactory<AppDbContext>();\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Better SQL for Optional One-to-One Joins\\n\\nEF Core now generates more efficient SQL for optional navigation properties in one-to-one relationships ([dotnet/efcore #37819](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37819)). Previously, EF Core emitted a `LEFT OUTER JOIN` for every optional to-one navigation even when the query shape made it unnecessary. EF Core 11 detects these cases and converts them to inner joins or eliminates the join entirely, reducing query cost without any model changes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## EF.Functions.JsonContains for SQL Server 2025\\n\\nA new `EF.Functions.JsonContains` translation targets the `JSON_CONTAINS` function available in SQL Server 2025 ([dotnet/efcore #37714](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37714)):\\n\\n```csharp\\n// Find products whose Tags JSON array contains \\\"sale\\\"\\nvar saleProducts = await context.Products\\n    .Where(p => EF.Functions.JsonContains(p.Tags, \\\"[\\\\\\\"sale\\\\\\\"]\\\"))\\n    .ToListAsync();\\n```\\n\\nRequires SQL Server 2025 or later; the provider throws at runtime on earlier versions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Exclude Foreign Keys from Migrations\\n\\nForeign key constraints can now be excluded from migrations individually ([dotnet/efcore #37815](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37815)). This is useful when FK constraints are enforced at the application layer or managed by another tooling, such as on read replicas or in legacy schemas:\\n\\n```csharp\\nmodelBuilder.Entity<Order>()\\n    .HasOne(o => o.Customer)\\n    .WithMany(c => c.Orders)\\n    .HasForeignKey(o => o.CustomerId)\\n    .ExcludeFromMigrations();\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## RemoveExtension and RemoveDbContext APIs\\n\\n`DbContextOptionsBuilder` now exposes `RemoveExtension<TExtension>()` and `RemoveDbContext()` ([dotnet/efcore #37891](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37891)). These are primarily useful in integration tests and multi-tenant scenarios where a pre-configured builder needs to be reconfigured:\\n\\n```csharp\\n// In a test fixture: swap the production SQL Server provider for SQLite\\nvar options = productionOptions\\n    .WithRemoveExtension<SqlServerOptionsExtension>()\\n    .UseSqlite(\\\"Data Source=test.db\\\")\\n    .Options;\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Migration ID Recorded in Model Snapshot\\n\\nThe model snapshot file now records the ID of the migration that generated it ([dotnet/efcore #37689](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/pull/37689)). This makes it easier to correlate a snapshot with its migration history and diagnose out-of-sync state in source control.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Community Contributors\\n\\nThank you to all community contributors who had PRs merged in EF Core in .NET 11 Preview 3:\\n\\n<!-- Community contributors will be listed here once the full PR list is available from the release tooling. -->\\n\", \"path\": \"/home/runner/work/dotnet-core-release-notes/dotnet-core-release-notes/release-notes/11.0/preview/preview3/efcore.md\"}"
